# Rule: Assertion Validity

An assertion that cannot fail proves nothing. Before trusting a green result, prove the
check is capable of going red.

## 1. Never assert an expected value that equals the current value

If the value you expect after a mutation is already what the record holds, the assertion
passes whether or not the code under test ran.

```
# Vacuous — baseline is already the expected result
baseline:  tag_a|tag_b
sent:      tag_a|tag_b|tag_a
expected:  tag_a|tag_b     ← passes even with the fix reverted

# Valid — the row must change for the assertion to hold
baseline:  tag_a|tag_b
sent:      tag_c|tag_d
expected:  tag_c|tag_d
```

Pick fixtures and payloads so that **expected ≠ baseline**. Where it adds signal, assert
the negative too: that the stored value is *not* the raw input.

## 2. Absence assertions need a negative control

A query that returns zero rows may be correct, or may be querying the wrong table, column,
or a typo'd name. Before accepting "no rows means clean", prove the same query returns rows
*somewhere* — another environment, another record, a pre-mutation snapshot.

**A search that returns nothing is an absence assertion too**, and free-text search is the
worst place to make one. Never conclude "X does not exist" from a query over human-written
text — titles, commit messages, page names, issue summaries. Find the authoritative record
instead.

**Corollary — do not infer a shared system's state from a local mirror.** A cached number,
a coverage file, or a local summary does not say which individual items are counted.
Read the authoritative source before reporting a delta.

## 3. Verify the premise before you write it into a ticket

Statements handed to you — "the script already ran", "that record doesn't exist", "it's
deployed" — are inputs to verify, not facts to relay. Check them against the database or
the deploy record before they enter a ticket comment, because that is the first thing a
reader will re-run.

Applies equally to your own earlier conclusions. If a later observation contradicts
something you already reported, say so explicitly and correct it rather than letting both
statements stand.

## 4. Test the explanation — don't just adopt it

When someone offers a cause, design the check that could refute it. A hypothesis that
survives an attempt to break it is worth reporting; one that was merely plausible is not.

Prefer an A/B on a single differing attribute over a sequence of one-off observations.

## 5. A silent no-op is not a pass

Some integrations accept malformed input, return success, and do nothing. Before reading
"no change" as evidence about the code under test, confirm the request was actually
processed.

When a check has an "or skip" variant, remember a skip is not a pass — audit periodically
that skips are environmental, not a broken payload or a wrong identifier.

**A helper keyword can manufacture the same false pass.** If a success check keys on
something that both success and failure produce (e.g. a button state), the check is
worthless. Key success on something only success produces.

## 6. One mutation, one observation window

Asynchronous verification reads a value some time after triggering a change. That reading
is only evidence if nothing *else* could have written the value in between.

- Fire one mutation, poll until you see its result, confirm the pipeline has drained,
  only then fire the next.
- If a result you already observed later disagrees with the current value, do not report
  either reading until you can explain the sequence. Re-run the case in isolation.
